== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

Thomason’s model structure equips the category of small categories with the homotopy theory of spaces.
  Its higher-dimensional analogue asks for the same result on the entire category of strict globular
  $n$-categories, with weak equivalences detected by the Street nerve and fibrations detected by its
  twice-extended nerve. We prove this analogue in every dimension,
  resolving positively the higher-dimensional Thomason model-structure conjecture of Ara and Maltsiniotis.

Fix a universe of small sets and enlarge the universe when forming categories of small objects.
  For a positive integer $n$, let $n upright("Cat")$ be the category of small strict globular $n$-categories and
  strict functors. We regard it as the full subcategory of $omega
  upright("Cat")$ consisting of strict $omega$-categories whose cells above dimension $n$ are identities; for $n =
  oo$, set $n upright("Cat") = omega upright("Cat")$. There is no invertibility assumption on positive-dimensional
  cells. The Street nerve and its left adjoint are denoted by

$ c_n : upright("sSet") arrows.rl n upright("Cat") : N_n \, wide \( N_n X \)_m =
  "Hom"_(omega upright("Cat")) \( cal(O)_m \, X \) \, $

where $cal(O)_m$ is the $m$th oriental. With the usual subdivision adjunction $upright("Sd") tack.l upright("Ex")$, put

$ L_n = c_n upright("Sd")^2 \, wide R_n = upright("Ex")^2 N_n . $

Write $W_(upright("KQ"))$ and $upright("Fib")_(upright("KQ"))$ for the weak homotopy equivalences and Kan
  fibrations of simplicial sets, and define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.1)")
 $ W_n = R_n^(- 1) \( W_(upright("KQ")) \) = N_n^(- 1) \( W_(upright("KQ")) \) \, wide F_n =
   R_n^(- 1) \( upright("Fib")_(upright("KQ")) \) \, wide C_n = zws^perp \( F_n ∩ W_n \) . $ <eq:prescribed-classes>]

Here $zws^perp$ denotes the left lifting class. The equality defining $W_n$ follows from the classical properties
  of $upright("Ex")$, recalled in Section 7.

#thm([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[For every $n in { 1 \, 2 \, 3 \, dots.h } union { oo }$,
    the classes $\( C_n \, W_n \, F_n \)$ in (1.1) form a proper combinatorial model structure on $n
    upright("Cat")$. It is cofibrantly generated by $\( L_n I \, L_n J \)$, where
$ I = { partial Delta \[ m \] arrow.r.hook Delta \[ m \] : m gt.eq 0 } \, wide J =
  { Lambda^k \[ m \] arrow.r.hook Delta \[ m \] : m gt.eq 1 \, med 0 lt.eq k lt.eq m } . $

 Moreover,
$ L_n : upright("sSet")_(upright("KQ")) arrows.rl \( n upright("Cat") \, C_n \, W_n \, F_n \) : R_n $

 is a Quillen equivalence.]
] <thm:main>

=== History and significance <history-and-significance>

Thomason established the case $n = 1$ by transferring the simplicial model structure through $c_1 upright("Sd")^2
  tack.l upright("Ex")^2 N_1$ #cite(label("Thomason1980")).
  His proof separates two issues that persist in higher dimensions:
  the homotopy behavior of the adjunction unit and the comparison between a pushout of nerves and the nerve of a
  categorical pushout. For the latter he introduced Dwyer maps and proved the comparison for their arbitrary
  cobase changes #cite(label("Thomason1980")) Definition 4.1 and Proposition 4.3.
  Cisinski corrected the retract argument used for properness,
  while preserving the model structure and its properness #cite(label("Cisinski1999")).
  Street’s orientals extend the nerve to strict higher categories #cite(label("Street1987")),
  providing the weak equivalences in the question considered here.

Ara and Maltsiniotis developed an abstract transfer theorem,
  drawing on the homotopy theory of categories developed by Grothendieck and Cisinski,
  and established the proper combinatorial model structure in dimension two.
  Using Chiche’s comparison theorem for the homotopy categories of categories and $2$-categories,
  they also obtained the Quillen equivalence #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2014")) Theorems 4.11, 6.27, 6.30,
  and Corollary 6.32. Worytkiewicz, Hess, Parent, and Tonks had proposed the two-dimensional structure earlier
  #cite(label("WorytkiewiczHessParentTonks2007")); their later corrigendum addresses the gaps identified by Ara
  and Maltsiniotis #cite(label("WorytkiewiczHessParentTonks2016")).
  For arbitrary $1 lt.eq n lt.eq oo$, the transfer framework isolates two sufficient conditions:
  a unit comparison on nerves of posets, and preservation of certain poset equivalences by every categorical
  pushout #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2014")) Scholie 5.14.

Ara and Maltsiniotis proved the poset-unit condition in all these dimensions #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2015"))
  Corollary 10.11 and Scholie 10.12. Gagna subsequently gave a direct simplicial proof and showed that the Street
  nerve induces an equivalence with homotopy types after localizing at the nerve weak equivalences
  #cite(label("Gagna2018")) Theorems 5.6 and 6.9. Thus strict higher categories already described the homotopy
  category of spaces in every dimension. The prescribed model structure requires the additional lifting and
  factorization statements, whose construction depends on controlling actual categorical pushouts.
  Ara’s 2023 account records this remaining sufficient condition and the general conjecture
  #cite(label("Ara2023")) §§9.2.5–9.2.6; Guetta and Maltsiniotis also state the $omega$-dimensional
  model-structure conjecture explicitly #cite(label("GuettaMaltsiniotis2024")) Introduction, p. 6.

The shapes of higher cells matter in this question. Fiore and Paoli constructed a Thomason model structure on
  small $n$-fold categories, whose weak equivalences are detected by the diagonal of the $n$-fold nerve and which
  is Quillen equivalent to simplicial sets #cite(label("FiorePaoli2010")) Theorem 9.28.
  Their objects are obtained by iterating the internal-category construction;
  the present theorem concerns strict globular categories and the Street nerve.

Theorem 1 supplies both weak factorization systems on all strict $n$-categories,
  together with left and right properness, by establishing the remaining pushout condition.
  Its arbitrary-target scope is essential: the small object argument must allow attachments to categories that
  need not be cofibrant or freely generated. The $omega$-dimensional proof uses finite orientals directly,
  and finite-dimensional reflection carries the same construction to each finite $n$.

=== Stationary cylinders and the pushout argument <stationary-cylinders-and-the-pushout-argument>

For a poset $E$, write $U_E = c_n N E$, where $N$ is the ordinary nerve.
  The principal intermediate result is Theorem 16: if $A subset.eq
  E$ is a downward-closed full subposet and its inclusion has a right adjoint retraction,
  then every pushout of $U_A arrow.r U_E$ is a Street weak equivalence.
  This proves the sufficient condition isolated in #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2014")) Scholie 5.14,
  condition (d$'$). The cylinders used for contraction fix $U_A$ throughout the interval.
  After any attachment $U_A arrow.r X$, this equality lets each cylinder glue to the constant cylinder on $X$,
  controlling the entire pushout and its newly formed composites.

The construction starts with a restricted but explicit directed prism.
  Suppose $f \, g : E arrow.r E$ are monotone, $f lt.eq g$, they agree on a sieve $A$,
  and $g$ is constant on $E \\ A$. Write $u$ for the degree-one generator of the interval chain complex.
  For every increasing tuple $w$, the formula

$ \[ w_0 \, dots.h \, w_p \] ⊗ u mapsto \[ f w_0 \, dots.h \, f w_p \, g w_p \] $

together with the endpoint maps induced by $f$ and $g$ defines a positive chain map after normalized repetitions
  are set to zero. It produces a strict Gray cylinder constant in the interval direction on $U_A$,
  at the common endpoint restriction induced by $f \|_A = g \|_A$.
  Its positivity is a consequence of the precise hypotheses on the old part and the constant upper map.
  Section 3 proves the chain identity, compatibility with all simplex operators, and descent to every finite dimension.

When $E \\ A$ has a greatest element, we choose two such cylinders whose defining poset maps restrict to
  $upright("id")_A$. They give a relative contraction by a zigzag, which extends across any attachment.
  For a general finite poset, deleting distinct maximal new elements gives two sieves containing $A$.
  The corresponding pushouts are full sieve subcategories of the total attachment,
  with intersection the pushout for the common subposet. Their nerves form an ordinary pushout along
  monomorphisms, so the local weak-equivalence statements glue.
  This allows each local contraction to be chosen independently.
  Finite subposets stable under the retraction then yield the result for arbitrary posets.

The remaining comparison follows the geometry of Thomason’s Dwyer maps #cite(label("Thomason1980"))
  Propositions 4.2–4.3. Two subdivisions put each generating boundary or horn inclusion into the form $N A
  arrow.r.hook N B$, where $A$ is a sieve and its inclusion into its upward closure in $B$ has a right adjoint
  retraction (Lemma 19). For any small poset pair with these properties,
  Proposition 18 provides a reusable comparison between the pushout of Street nerves and the nerve of an
  attachment along an arbitrary functor $U_A arrow.r X$. The poset description here concerns the generating pieces.

At the chain level we use Steiner’s description of orientals #cite(label("Steiner2004"))
  #cite(label("Steiner2007")) and the biclosed Gray tensor product,
  with the completed extension theorem of Ara and Maltsiniotis #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2020")) Theorem A.15.
  Positive-chain constructions already give simplicial nerve homotopies in Gagna’s proof #cite(label("Gagna2018"))
  Sections 6.3–6.8. Here the restricted prism hypotheses give strict relative cylinders;
  the choices fixing $A$ extend across arbitrary attachments.
  The cover calculations build on the fiber and sieve machinery of Ara and Maltsiniotis
  #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2014")) Lemma 2.8 and Propositions 5.3 and 5.5;
  they allow us to glue the weak-equivalence conclusions without choosing compatible contractions on overlapping pieces.

=== Organization <organization>

Section 2 records the foundations and the precise simplicial gluing lemma.
  Sections 3 and 4 construct the stationary cylinders and identify labelled pieces.
  Section 5 proves couniversality and the unit comparison for posets.
  Section 6 proves the nerve comparison for subdivided boundary and horn attachments,
  with explicit control of the monomorphic gluing legs. Section 7 supplies the model axioms, properness,
  and the unit comparison for arbitrary simplicial sets. Appendix 8 proves the required presentability and
  smallness assertions by guarded term presentations. Appendix 9 discusses the relationship with the
  elementary-expansion method for Grothendieck groupoids; its claims are not inputs to the proof.

== 2 Strict categories, directed chains, and simplicial gluing <sec:foundations>

The pushout argument needs three foundations: directed chain maps must give strict right cylinders,
  these cylinders must descend to finite dimensions, and their nerve comparisons must survive gluing and filtered
  colimits. We fix the conventions and prove the needed local statements before constructing the relative prism.

We work with small objects in a fixed universe. An $omega$-category in this paper is a strict globular
  $omega$-category: its cells have globular sources and targets, identities,
  and compositions along matching lower-dimensional boundaries, satisfying the associativity, unit,
  and interchange laws strictly. An $n$-category, for finite $n$, has only identity cells above dimension $n$.
  The inclusion $n upright("Cat") arrow.r.hook omega upright("Cat")$ has a left adjoint $tau_n$.
  Explicitly, $tau_n X$ retains the cells of $X$ in dimensions below $n$,
  and its $n$-cells are the $n$-cells of $X$ modulo the congruence generated by

$ s_n \( z \) = t_n \( z \) wide \( z upright(" an ") \( n + 1 \) upright("-cell of ") X \) . $

Here the congruence is closed under every defined composition.
  Its generating pairs are parallel $n$-cells, so their identifications leave every lower matching boundary
  unchanged; compositions therefore descend to the quotient. Cells above $n$ become identities.
  Every strict functor from $X$ to an $n$-category annihilates these relations,
  which proves the stated reflection property. We set $tau_oo = upright("id")$ and use $n upright("Cat") =
  omega upright("Cat")$ when $n = oo$.

Write $D_i$ for the free strict $omega$-category on one $i$-cell, with all its globular faces.
  Thus $"Hom"_(omega upright("Cat")) \( D_i \, X \)$ is the set of $i$-cells of $X$, $D_0$ is terminal,
  and $D_1$ is the category $0 arrow.r 1$. Let $cal(O)_m$ denote the $m$th oriental.
  Its standard cosimplicial structure defines

$ \( N_n X \)_m = "Hom"_(omega upright("Cat")) \( cal(O)_m \, X \) =
  "Hom"_(n upright("Cat")) \( tau_n cal(O)_m \, X \) . $

For $omega$-categories we also write $N_oo$. The left adjoints are given by the simplex colimits

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.1)")
 $ c_oo K = "colim"_(\( Delta \[ m \] arrow.r K \) in Delta \/ K) cal(O)_m \, wide c_n K = tau_n c_oo K =
   "colim"_(\( Delta \[ m \] arrow.r K \) in Delta \/ K) tau_n cal(O)_m . $ <eq:categorification-colimit>]

These formulas follow from the universal property of a colimit and the degreewise definition of the nerve.
  In particular, for a poset $E$,

$ U_E^oo = c_oo N E \, wide U_E = c_n N E = tau_n U_E^oo . $

The fixed dimension is suppressed in $U$: for a monotone map $f$, write $U \( f \) =
  c_n N \( f \)$ and $U^oo \( f \) = c_oo N \( f \)$. We use $N$ for the ordinary nerve.
  On ordinary categories it agrees with the Street nerve: a map from an oriental is determined by its successive
  edges, because its triangular cells impose the usual composition equations. In particular $N_oo D_1 = Delta \[ 1 \]$.

=== 2.1 The directed tensor convention <the-directed-tensor-convention>

An augmented directed complex is a nonnegative chain complex $K$ of abelian groups,
  with augmentation $epsilon.alt : K_0 arrow.r bb(Z)$ and a specified additive submonoid $K_p^(+) subset
  K_p$ in each degree. Its morphisms are chain maps preserving the augmentation and these submonoids.
  All complexes used below have a specified free basis, with positive submonoids generated by the basis.
  For such a complex write $d c = d^(+) c - d^(-) c$ with disjoint positive supports.
  A basis is #emph[unital] when $epsilon.alt \( \( d^(-) \)^p b \) = epsilon.alt \( \( d^(+) \)^p b \) =
  1$ for each basis element $b$ of degree $p$, and is #emph[strongly loop-free] when the relations

$ a < b quad upright("if ") a upright(" occurs in ") d^(-) b \, wide b < a quad
  upright("if ") a upright(" occurs in ") d^(+) b $

are contained in a strict partial order on the whole basis.
  A complex with such a basis is called a #emph[strong Steiner complex].

Steiner’s functor $nu$ sends augmented directed complexes to strict $omega$-categories.
  Its cells retain the lower-dimensional boundary data of a chain. More concretely, a $p$-cell of $nu K$ is a table

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.2)")
 $ \( x_0^(-) \, x_0^(+) divides x_1^(-) \, x_1^(+) divides dots.h.c divides x_p^(-) \, x_p^(+) \) \, wide
   x_r^plus.minus in K_r^(+) \, $ <eq:steiner-cell-table>]

such that

$ epsilon.alt x_0^(-) = epsilon.alt x_0^(+) = 1 \, wide d x_r^(-) = d x_r^(+) = x_(r - 1)^(+) -
  x_(r - 1)^(-) quad \( 1 lt.eq r lt.eq p \) \, wide x_p^(-) = x_p^(+) . $

An identity appends a zero pair to the table. A positive, augmentation-preserving chain map acts on every entry,
  giving the corresponding strict functor #cite(label("Steiner2004")) Definitions 2.6 and 2.8, Proposition 2.7.
  For a basis element $b$ of degree $p$, its #emph[atom] has top pair $\( b \, b \)$ and entries $chevron.l b
  chevron.r_r^plus.minus = \( d^plus.minus \)^(p - r) b$ for $0 lt.eq r lt.eq p$:
  at each step, take the negative or positive part of the boundary.
  For a strong Steiner complex these tables are cells, and $nu
  K$ is freely generated as a polygraph by the atoms of its basis.
  In particular, maps out of it are determined by the images of these atoms #cite(label("Steiner2004")) §3 before
  Definition 3.2, Theorem 6.1. The concrete complexes needed here are as follows:

+ The normalized simplex complex $M \[ m \]$ has degree-$p$ basis $\[ i_0 \, dots.h \, i_p \]$ for $0 lt.eq
  i_0 < dots.h.c < i_p lt.eq m$, with
  $ d \[ i_0 \, dots.h \, i_p \] = sum_(j = 0)^p \( - 1 \)^j \[ i_0 \, dots.h \, hat(i_j) \, dots.h \, i_p \]
    quad \( p > 0 \) \, wide epsilon.alt \[ i \] = 1 . $
  It is strong Steiner and $nu M \[ m \] = cal(O)_m$. A monotone map of ordinals acts by applying it to the tuple;
    a tuple with a repeated entry is zero. The atom of a nonempty tuple has its first and last vertices as its
    $0$-source and $0$-target.
+ The globe complex $G \[ i \]$ has one top basis element in degree $i$ and two basis elements,
  its source and target faces, in each degree below $i$. Its positive-degree differentials are target minus
  source, and its vertices have augmentation one. It is strong Steiner and $nu G \[ i \] = D_i$.
  For $i = 0$ there is just one vertex.

The strong Steiner properties and tensor closure follow from #cite(label("Steiner2004")) Examples 3.8–3.10.
  The disk identification follows from #cite(label("Steiner2004")) Example 4.7 and Theorem 5.11.
  For the oriental identification and its cosimplicial action, including repeated entries,
  see #cite(label("Steiner2007")) Theorem 3.2 and §4 before Theorem 4.1.

The tensor of augmented directed complexes uses the ordinary nonnegative chain-complex tensor,
  with separate augmentation:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.3)")
 $ d \( x ⊗ y \) = d x ⊗ y + \( - 1 \)^(\| x \|) x ⊗ d y \, wide epsilon.alt \( x ⊗ y \) =
   epsilon.alt \( x \) epsilon.alt \( y \) quad \( \| x \| = \| y \| = 0 \) . $ <eq:tensor-differential>]

Its positive submonoid is generated by the tensors of positive elements.
  Tensor products of the indicated bases are the relevant positive bases.

We use the biclosed Gray tensor product on #emph[all] strict $omega$-categories supplied by
  #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2020")) Theorem A.15 and §A.16.
  It has unit $D_0$, preserves small colimits separately in each variable, and has canonical natural identifications

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.4)")
 $ nu K ⊗ nu L med tilde.equiv med nu \( K ⊗ L \) wide \( K \, L upright(" strong Steiner") \) . $ <eq:steiner-tensor>]

Naturality here is for every morphism between strong Steiner complexes,
  including maps sending basis elements to sums or zero. This is the completed extension theorem;
  the general extension is not being inferred from the incomplete proof of #cite(label("Steiner2004"))
  Theorem 7.3. In particular, preservation of colimits in this paper is a property of the Gray tensor,
  not a claimed preservation property of $nu$.

It follows that $D_i ⊗ D_1$ is generated by the atoms indexed by pairs of globe basis elements,
  of dimension the sum of their dimensions. The same holds for $cal(O)_m ⊗ D_1$.
  Globe face inclusions and simplex face inclusions identify the corresponding based subcomplexes,
  and hence the corresponding atoms in these tensor products.
  One way to see the last assertion directly is to compute the positive and negative boundaries of a basis element
  within the based subcomplex: they are unchanged by its inclusion.
  The atom construction therefore gives the same cell in the larger complex.

Throughout, the interval is the #emph[right] tensor factor.
  Write $e_0 \, e_1$ for the degree-zero basis elements of $M \[ 1 \]$ and $u$ for its degree-one element,
  so that $d u = e_1 - e_0$. Also write $e_a : D_0 arrow.r D_1$ for its endpoint functors and $epsilon.alt :
  D_1 arrow.r D_0$ for the unique functor. The projection is

$ pi_X = upright("id")_X ⊗ epsilon.alt : X ⊗ D_1 arrow.r X ⊗ D_0 tilde.equiv X . $

No interchange of the two tensor factors is used.

=== 2.2 Right cylinders and truncation <right-cylinders-and-truncation>

Biclosedness supplies an $omega$-category $H \( Y \)$ and natural bijections

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.5)")
 $ "Hom"_(omega upright("Cat")) \( X \, H \( Y \) \) tilde.equiv "Hom"_(omega upright("Cat")) \( X ⊗ D_1 \, Y \) .
   $ <eq:right-internal-hom>]

In the notation of #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2020")) §A.18, $H \( Y \) =
  "Hom"_(upright("oplax")) \( D_1 \, Y \)$; the representing formula (2.5) fixes the convention.
  Evaluation at $e_0 \, e_1$ and precomposition with $pi_X$ give, respectively,
  the endpoints and constant cylinders. To extend cylinders through pushouts in $n upright("Cat")$,
  we will transpose them to functors into $H \( Y \)$. The next lemma shows that this construction stays within $n
  upright("Cat")$ when the target $Y$ does.

#thm([Lemma 2.1.])[
  (Internal hom and reflection).  #emph[Let $n < oo$ and let $Y$ be a strict $n$-category.
    Then $H \( Y \)$ is a strict $n$-category. Consequently the reflection unit $X arrow.r
    tau_n X$ induces a natural bijection
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.6)")
 $ "Hom"_(omega upright("Cat")) \( \( tau_n X \) ⊗ D_1 \, Y \) med arrow.r^(med tilde.equiv med) med
   "Hom"_(omega upright("Cat")) \( X ⊗ D_1 \, Y \) . $ <eq:truncated-cylinder-bijection>]

 This bijection respects endpoints and restriction along functors in the first variable.
   For $n = oo$ the same assertion holds with $tau_n = upright("id")$.]
] <lem:internal-truncation>

#proof[
  We prove the special case needed here directly; the general internal-hom truncation assertion is also
    #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2020")) Proposition A.29. An $i$-cell of $H \( Y \)$ is a functor $P :
    D_i ⊗ D_1 arrow.r Y$. Let $s : D_(i - 1) arrow.r D_i$ be the source face inclusion, and let $p : D_i arrow.r
    D_(i - 1)$ send the top $i$-cell to the identity of the top $\( i - 1 \)$-cell.
    Thus $p s = upright("id")$ and $q = s p$ collapses $D_i$ onto its source face.
    Under (2.5), the identity of the source of $P$ is represented by $P \( q ⊗ upright("id") \)$.
    We show that these functors agree when $i > n$.
  The atoms of $D_i ⊗ D_1$ outside the source face $D_(i - 1) ⊗ D_1$ come from exactly the following pairs:
    the top $i$-generator or the target $\( i - 1 \)$-generator of $D_i$, paired with $e_0 \, e_1$, or $u$.
    Their dimensions are respectively $i \, i \, i + 1$ and $i - 1 \, i - 1 \, i$.
    If $i > n + 1$, all have dimension greater than $n$. If $i = n + 1$,
    only the target $n$-generator paired with $e_0$ or $e_1$ has dimension at most $n$.
    At either endpoint, the top $\( n + 1 \)$-cell maps under $P$ to an identity,
    so its source and target $n$-cells have the same image. These are precisely the two equalities needed to
    compare $P$ with $P \( q ⊗ upright("id") \)$ on the remaining degree-$n$ atoms.
    All atoms in the source face are fixed by $q ⊗ upright("id")$.
  The two functors therefore agree on all atoms of dimension at most $n$. They agree on higher atoms as well:
    in an $n$-category the image of a cell of dimension greater than $n$ must be the iterated identity on its
    $n$-source, whose image has already been determined. Generation by atoms proves $P =
    P \( q ⊗ upright("id") \)$. Every cell of $H \( Y \)$ above $n$ is thus an identity.
  Now transpose a cylinder to a functor $X arrow.r H \( Y \)$.
    Since $H \( Y \)$ is an $n$-category, it factors uniquely through $tau_n X$. Transposing back proves (2.6).
    Naturality of the reflection and of the adjunction proves the restriction assertion.
    The endpoint assertions follow either by naturality of evaluation or by uniqueness of the factorization into $Y$. ◻
]

#prodef([Remark 2.2.])[
  Even when $X$ and $Y$ are $n$-categories, the expression $X ⊗ D_1$ denotes their tensor in $omega
    upright("Cat")$; it can have cells above $n$. A map from this tensor to $Y$ is nevertheless represented by a
    map $X arrow.r H \( Y \)$ #emph[in $n upright("Cat")$]. This is the fact that permits stationary cylinders to
    be glued by arbitrary pushouts in $n upright("Cat")$.
] <rem:omega-tensor-at-finite-n>

=== 2.3 The Alexander–Whitney map <the-alexanderwhitney-map>

For the Gray-cylinder-to-Street-nerve construction, see Ara #cite(label("Ara2023")) Sections 5.2.2–5.2.6,
  especially Proposition 5.2.3 and the right-cylinder formula in 5.2.6.
  The verification needed here is included below; the classical Alexander–Whitney diagonal is credited separately
  in the proof.

#thm([Lemma 2.3.])[
  (A right Gray cylinder gives a nerve homotopy).  #emph[There is a natural simplicial map
$ J_X : N_oo X times Delta \[ 1 \] arrow.r N_oo \( X ⊗ D_1 \) $

 whose restriction at the endpoint $a$ is $N_oo \( upright("id")_X ⊗ e_a \)$,
   and for which $N_oo \( pi_X \) J_X$ is the projection to $N_oo X$.
   Consequently a strict functor $P : X ⊗ D_1 arrow.r Y$ induces a simplicial homotopy between the nerves of its
   endpoint functors. If a functor $a : A arrow.r X$ satisfies $P \( a ⊗ upright("id") \) = h pi_A$ for some $h :
   A arrow.r Y$, the homotopy restricts along $N_oo a$ to the constant homotopy of $N_oo h$.
   The same assertions apply to $N_n$ when $X \, Y$ are $n$-categories.]
] <lem:gray-nerve>

#proof[
  For each $m$ define the classical Alexander–Whitney diagonal #cite(label("GoerssJardine1999")) IV.2, Remark 2.5
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.7)")
   $ "AW"_m \[ i_0 \, dots.h \, i_p \] = sum_(a = 0)^p \[ i_0 \, dots.h \, i_a \] ⊗ \[ i_a \, dots.h \, i_p \] .
     $ <eq:aw>]
  It is a positive chain map $M \[ m \] arrow.r M \[ m \] ⊗ M \[ m \]$.
    For clarity, in the differential of the right side, deleting a vertex strictly before or after the shared
    vertex gives the corresponding term in $"AW"_m d$, with coefficient $\( - 1 \)^j$ for deletion of $i_j$.
    The remaining terms cancel in pairs: deleting the final vertex of the first factor at cut $a$ contributes
  $ \( - 1 \)^a \[ i_0 \, dots.h \, i_(a - 1) \] ⊗ \[ i_a \, dots.h \, i_p \] \, $
  whereas deleting the initial vertex of the second factor at cut $a -
    1$ contributes the same tensor with coefficient $\( - 1 \)^(a - 1)$.
    These cancellations use exactly the sign in (2.3). The degree-zero assertion is immediate.
    Every summand in (2.7) is positive. On vertices the augmentation is one;
    applying the augmentation to the first or second tensor factor selects, respectively, only the cut $a =
    0$ or $a = p$. Thus both counits give the identity.
  The diagonal is natural for every monotone ordinal map. For a tuple whose image is strictly increasing,
    this follows term by term. If the image has equal consecutive entries in positions $j \, j + 1$,
    then at every cut $a lt.eq j$ the second factor is degenerate, and at every cut $a gt.eq j +
    1$ the first factor is degenerate. Both sides of naturality are therefore zero after normalization.
    This verifies degeneracies as well as faces. Only this cosimplicial naturality is asserted.
  Apply $nu$ and (2.4) to obtain natural functors $delta_m : cal(O)_m arrow.r cal(O)_m ⊗ cal(O)_m$.
    For an $m$-simplex $F : cal(O)_m arrow.r X$ and a monotone map $theta : \[ m \] arrow.r \[ 1 \]$, put
  $ J_X \( F \, theta \) = \( F ⊗ cal(O)_theta \) delta_m \, $
  where $cal(O)_theta : cal(O)_m arrow.r cal(O)_1 = D_1$ is the cosimplicial structure map.
    The naturality just checked makes $J_X$ simplicial. If $theta$ is constant at $a$,
    this map factors through the right counit and the endpoint $e_a$, giving the stated endpoint formula.
    Applying $pi_X$ also gives the right counit, so $N_oo \( pi_X \) J_X$ is the projection.
    Postcomposing with $N_oo P$ proves the homotopy assertion,
    and naturality in $X$ together with the projection formula proves stationarity. ◻
]

=== 2.4 Finiteness and gluing in simplicial sets <finiteness-and-gluing-in-simplicial-sets>

We use the Kan–Quillen model structure on $upright("sSet")$.
  Its cofibrations are the monomorphisms, every object is cofibrant, and the model structure is proper.
  We also use the classical Kan replacement $K arrow.r upright("Ex")^oo K$,
  which is a weak equivalence and has Kan target. These are simplicial homotopy-theory inputs;
  they do not presuppose any model structure on strict higher categories #cite(label("GoerssJardine1999")) II.8.6
  and III.4.8.

#thm([Lemma 2.4.])[
  (Presentability and filtered colimits).  #emph[For every $1 lt.eq n lt.eq oo$,
    the category $n upright("Cat")$ is locally finitely presentable, and filtered colimits are computed on cells.
    Each $tau_n cal(O)_m$ is finitely presentable in $n upright("Cat")$.
    Consequently $N_n$, and also $upright("Ex")^r N_n$ for every finite $r gt.eq 0$, preserve filtered colimits.
    Simplicial weak equivalences are closed under filtered colimits of arrows,
    as are the maps $f$ of $n upright("Cat")$ for which $N_n f$ is a weak equivalence.
    No monomorphism hypothesis on transition maps is required.]
] <lem:filtered>

#proof[
  Strict $n$-categories are models of a small finitary essentially algebraic theory.
    The sorts are the cell dimensions; the operations are source, target, identity, and the binary compositions.
    A composition is defined by finitely many matching-boundary equations,
    and every strict category axiom is an equation involving finitely many cells.
    In the $omega$-case there are countably many sorts, but all operations and equations still involve finite
    data. Proposition 30 proves local finite presentability, completeness,
    cocompleteness and a common regular presentability bound directly from this signature.
    Its presentation construction includes composites made possible by new boundary identifications.
    The general essentially algebraic approach is developed in #cite(label("AdamekRosicky1994"))
    #cite(label("AdamekHebertRosicky1999")); for its finitary partial-operation formulation,
    see also #cite(label("HebertAdamekRosicky2001")) pp. 52–53.
  For the cellwise colimit assertion one can argue directly.
    Form the filtered colimit of the sets of cells in each dimension.
    Any finite collection of representatives is represented at a common stage.
    If their boundaries agree in the colimit, those finitely many equalities hold at some later common stage,
    where the required composition is defined. Define its colimit value by that composition.
    A further common stage proves independence of choices, and each strict category law follows at a stage
    containing its finitely many entries. This constructs the colimit with its universal property.
    If all inputs are $n$-categories, every cell above $n$ is already an identity, so the same is true of the colimit.
  The oriental $cal(O)_m$ has a finite polygraphic presentation:
    there is one generator for each nonempty strictly increasing tuple in $\[ m \]$,
    and its boundary is an expression in lower generators. A map from $cal(O)_m$ into a filtered colimit is thus
    specified by finitely many cell representatives satisfying finitely many boundary equations.
    These representatives and equations occur at one stage. Equality of two such maps is likewise detected at one
    stage. Hence $cal(O)_m$ is finitely presentable in $omega upright("Cat")$.
    The inclusion $n upright("Cat") arrow.r.hook omega upright("Cat")$ preserves filtered colimits by the
    preceding cellwise calculation, so reflection and adjunction give finite presentability of $tau_n cal(O)_m$.
    This proves the assertion for $N_n$ degreewise; compare also #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2014"))
    Proposition 5.13(a).
    Since $\( upright("Ex") K \)_m = "Hom"_(upright("sSet")) \( upright("Sd") Delta \[ m \] \, K \)$ and
    $upright("Sd") Delta \[ m \]$ is a finite simplicial set, $upright("Ex")$ preserves filtered colimits too.
  Here are details of the weak-equivalence assertion. The functor $Q =
    upright("Ex")^oo$ preserves filtered colimits, since $upright("Ex")$ does and colimits commute with colimits.
    A filtered colimit of Kan complexes is Kan: any horn has finitely many nondegenerate simplices,
    so it and its compatibility equations occur at one stage and can be filled there.
    For a filtered diagram of Kan complexes $K_i$, components commute with its colimit,
    since vertices and finite paths are finite data. If $x$ is a vertex of $"colim"_i K_i$,
    choose a representative $x_i in K_i$. The coslice of arrows $i arrow.r
    j$ is filtered and its projection to the indexing category is final;
    it gives a diagram with compatible basepoints $x_j$. For every $k gt.eq 1$,
  $ "colim"_(\( i arrow.r j \)) pi_k \( K_j \, x_j \) med tilde.equiv med pi_k \( "colim"_j K_j \, x \) . $
  Indeed, use the finite pointed sphere $Delta \[ k \] \/ partial
    Delta \[ k \]$ and its finite reduced simplicial cylinder to represent classes and pointed homotopies in a Kan
    complex. Their simplices and all required equalities, including the basepoint equality,
    occur at a sufficiently late stage. This proves both surjectivity and injectivity of the displayed map,
    including eventual equalities when transition maps are not injective.
    It follows that an objectwise weak equivalence between filtered diagrams of Kan complexes induces a weak
    equivalence of colimits. Apply this to $Q$ of a diagram of arbitrary simplicial weak equivalences,
    and use the natural weak equivalences $K arrow.r Q K$ and two-out-of-three.
    Finally apply preservation of filtered colimits by $N_n$. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 2.5.])[
  (Gluing with one monomorphic leg).  #emph[Consider a commutative map of spans of simplicial sets
#cetz.canvas({
  import cetz.draw: *
  content((0, 0), [$A$])
  content((2.189, 0), [$C$])
  content((4.445, 0), [$B$])
  content((0, -1.239), [$A'$])
  content((2.189, -1.239), [$C'$])
  content((4.445, -1.239), [$B' .$])
  line((0, -0.2725), (0, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((-0.3223, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$a$]])
  line((1.853, 0), (0.336, 0), mark: (end: ">"))
  line((2.525, 0), (4.109, 0), mark: (end: ">"))
  line((2.525, 0), (2.525, 0.3))
  content((3.317, 0.2266), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$i$]])
  line((2.189, -0.2725), (2.189, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((1.867, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$c$]])
  line((4.445, -0.2725), (4.445, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((4.767, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$b$]])
  line((1.787, -1.239), (0.402, -1.239), mark: (end: ">"))
  line((2.591, -1.239), (3.977, -1.239), mark: (end: ">"))
  line((2.591, -1.239), (2.591, -0.9386))
  content((3.284, -1.465), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$i'$]])
})

 where $i$ and $i'$ are monomorphisms. If $a \, b \, c$ are weak equivalences, then the canonical map
$ A product.co_C B arrow.r A' product.co_(C') B' $

 is a weak equivalence. There is no condition on the maps $C arrow.r A$ and $C' arrow.r A'$.
   Each of the displayed ordinary pushouts computes a homotopy pushout.
   In particular, cobase change of a weak equivalence along a simplicial monomorphism is a weak equivalence.]
] <lem:simplicial-gluing>

#proof[
  This is the simplicial gluing lemma #cite(label("GoerssJardine1999")) II.8.8 and II.8.12;
    the following proof keeps track of the specified cofibrations. Put
  $ E = B product.co_C C' . $
  By simplicial left properness, $B arrow.r E$ is a weak equivalence,
    since it is the pushout of $c$ along the monomorphism $i$.
    The induced map $E arrow.r B'$ is consequently a weak equivalence by two-out-of-three.
    Both $C' arrow.r E$ and $C' arrow.r B'$ are cofibrations.
    They are therefore cofibrant objects of the undercategory $C' \/ upright("sSet")$,
    with its inherited model structure. Cobase change along $C' arrow.r A'$ is a left Quillen functor
  $ C' \/ upright("sSet") arrow.r A' \/ upright("sSet") : quad \( C' arrow.r T \)
    mapsto \( A' arrow.r A' product.co_(C') T \) . $
  Indeed, its right adjoint is restriction of the structure map,
    which preserves fibrations and trivial fibrations. It preserves weak equivalences between cofibrant objects, so
  $ A' product.co_C B med tilde.equiv med A' product.co_(C') E arrow.r A' product.co_(C') B' $
  is a weak equivalence. Also $A arrow.r A product.co_C B$ is a monomorphism, being a pushout of $i$.
    Left properness applied to $a$ gives a weak equivalence $A product.co_C B arrow.r A' product.co_C B$.
    Composing the two proves the result.
  To see the homotopy-pushout assertion, factor the possibly nonmonomorphic map $C arrow.r
    A$ as a cofibration $C arrow.r.hook tilde(A)$ followed by a trivial fibration $tilde(A) arrow.r A$.
    The span $tilde(A) arrow.l C arrow.r.hook B$ is a cofibrant replacement of the original span for calculating
    the derived pushout: its three objects are cofibrant and both legs are cofibrations.
    The already proved assertion identifies its pushout with $A product.co_C B$ up to weak equivalence.
    For the final assertion, one may either use left properness directly or apply the span assertion to $X arrow.l
    X arrow.r.hook Z$ and $Y arrow.l X arrow.r.hook Z$, with vertical maps $X arrow.r Y \, upright("id")_X \,
    upright("id")_Z$. ◻
]

== 3 Positive relative prisms <sec:prisms>

Throughout this section the interval is the #emph[right] tensor factor.
  At the chain level this is the antihomotopy convention of #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2020"))
  Appendix B.4.1–B.4.2. Gagna constructs simplicial homotopies by positive concatenation of chains under an order
  condition on the endpoint maps #cite(label("Gagna2018")) Sections 6.3–6.8.
  We use a related positive formula under the sieve and constancy hypotheses below to construct a strict right
  Gray cylinder stationary on the entire old subcategory. When its endpoint maps fix that subcategory,
  it glues to the constant cylinder on an arbitrary attaching target.
  Write $epsilon.alt : D_1 arrow.r D_0$ for its unique map to the Gray unit, and write

$ pi_B = upright("id")_B ⊗ epsilon.alt : B ⊗ D_1 arrow.r B . $

For $i : A arrow.r B$ and $v : A arrow.r C$, a cylinder $P : B ⊗ D_1 arrow.r
  C$ is #emph[stationary along $i$ relative to $v$] if $P \( i ⊗ upright("id") \) = v pi_A$.
  This is an equality of strict functors, including every cell of $A$.
  The special prism below supplies this equality even when the common endpoint map on $A$ is not the inclusion.

#thm([Lemma 3.1.])[
  (Relative prism).  #emph[Let $A subset E$ be a downward-closed subposet, and let $f \, g : E arrow.r
    E$ be monotone maps such that
$ f \( e \) lt.eq g \( e \) quad \( e in E \) \, wide f \|_A = g \|_A \, wide g \|_(E \\ A) upright(" is constant") . $

 The constancy condition is vacuous when $E \\ A$ is empty.
   For each $1 lt.eq n lt.eq oo$ there is a strict functor of $omega$-categories
$ P_(f \, g) : U_E ⊗ D_1 arrow.r U_E $

 whose restrictions to the two interval endpoints are $U \( f \)$ and $U \( g \)$.
   If $i : A arrow.r.hook E$ and $v = f i = g i : A arrow.r E$, then
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.1)")
 $ P_(f \, g) \( U \( i \) ⊗ upright("id")_(D_1) \) = U \( v \) pi_(U_A) . $ <eq:prism-stationarity>]

 Consequently its endpoints induce simplicially homotopic maps on Street nerves,
   with the homotopy stationary relative to $N_n U \( v \)$.]
] <lem:relative-prism>

#proof[
  #emph[Simplexwise chain map.] We first construct the cylinder in $omega upright("Cat")$,
    before applying $tau_n$. If $T$ is a nonempty finite totally ordered set,
    let $M \[ T \]$ be its augmented normalized simplex chain complex.
    Thus its positive basis in degree $p$ consists of the strictly increasing tuples $\[ t_0 \, dots.h \, t_p \]$.
    A weakly increasing tuple with a repeated entry means zero.
    For $p > 0$ the differential is the alternating sum of deletions; the augmentation sends every vertex to $1$.
    The alternating deletion formula remains valid for a tuple with repetitions:
    it is the differential in the normalized quotient of the unnormalized simplex complex.
    In particular all calculations below remain valid when a displayed tuple is zero.
  Fix a simplex $sigma = \( v_0 lt.eq dots.h.c lt.eq v_m \)$ of $N E$ and put
  $ T_sigma = { f \( v_i \) \, g \( v_i \) : 0 lt.eq i lt.eq m } subset E . $
  This subset is totally ordered. Indeed, the indices whose vertices belong to $A$ form a prefix.
    On that prefix the two image lists coincide. If there are remaining vertices,
    all their $g$-images are one element $b$, and every $f \( v_i \)$ is at most $f \( v_m \) lt.eq b$.
    Consequently $T_sigma$ consists of the ordered $f$-image list, possibly with this one upper element adjoined.
    If every vertex is old, $T_sigma$ is just the $f$-image list.
    The order embedding $T_sigma arrow.r.hook E$ gives the canonical map
  $ nu M \[ T_sigma \] arrow.r U_E^oo . $
  Here $nu M \[ T_sigma \]$ is the oriental obtained by enumerating $T_sigma$ increasingly.
  Let $e_0 \, e_1$ denote the vertices and $u$ the edge of $M \[ 1 \]$, so $d u = e_1 - e_0$.
    We define a graded homomorphism
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.2)")
   $ C_sigma : M \[ m \] ⊗ M \[ 1 \] arrow.r M \[ T_sigma \] $ <eq:prism-chain-map>]
  on the tensor basis. For $x = \[ i_0 \, dots.h \, i_p \]$, with $i_0 < dots.h.c < i_p$, set $w_j =
    v_(i_j)$ and prescribe
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
   $ C_sigma \( x ⊗ e_0 \) & = F \( w \) := \[ f \( w_0 \) \, dots.h \, f \( w_p \) \] \,\
C_sigma \( x ⊗ e_1 \) & = G \( w \) := \[ g \( w_0 \) \, dots.h \, g \( w_p \) \] \,\
C_sigma \( x ⊗ u \) & = h \( w \) := \[ f \( w_0 \) \, dots.h \, f \( w_p \) \, g \( w_p \) \] . $ <eq:prism-basis>]
  Each image is a positive basis element or zero: all displayed tuples are weakly increasing in $T_sigma$.
    Both endpoint prescriptions are the normalized chain maps of monotone maps on vertices.
    They preserve augmentation, and hence so does $C_sigma$ in degree zero.
  It remains to check the differential on $x ⊗ u$, including its sign. If $p = 0$, the required identity is precisely
  $ d \[ f \( w_0 \) \, g \( w_0 \) \] = \[ g \( w_0 \) \] - \[ f \( w_0 \) \] . $
  For $p > 0$, abbreviate
  $ B & = \[ f \( w_0 \) \, dots.h \, f \( w_(p - 1) \) \, g \( w_p \) \] \,\
B' & = \[ f \( w_0 \) \, dots.h \, f \( w_(p - 1) \) \, g \( w_(p - 1) \) \] . $
  We claim that $B - B' = G \( w \)$ in normalized chains. If $w_(p - 1) in A$, all $w_0 \, dots.h \,
    w_(p - 1)$ lie in $A$, so that $B = G \( w \)$, whereas $B' = 0$ because its last two entries coincide.
    If $w_(p - 1) in.not A$, then also $w_p in.not A$. Constancy of $g$ on the complement gives $B =
    B'$ and makes the last two entries of $G \( w \)$ coincide, so again $B - B' = G \( w \)$.
    Expanding $d h \( w \)$ and canceling the terms obtained by deleting one of $f \( w_0 \) \, dots.h \,
    f \( w_(p - 1) \)$ therefore gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
   $ d h \( w \) & = sum_(j = 0)^p \( - 1 \)^j h \( w_0 \, dots.h \, hat(w_j) \, dots.h \, w_p \) + \( - 1 \)^p
     #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] B - B' - F \( w \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]\
 & = sum_(j = 0)^p \( - 1 \)^j h \( w_0 \, dots.h \, hat(w_j) \, dots.h \, w_p \) + \( - 1 \)^p
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] G \( w \) - F \( w \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:prism-boundary>]
  This is exactly the image under $C_sigma$ of
  $ d \( x ⊗ u \) = d x ⊗ u + \( - 1 \)^p x ⊗ \( e_1 - e_0 \) . $
  Thus (3.2) is a positive, augmentation-preserving chain map in every degree.
    The strong Steiner tensor comparison gives the strict functor
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.5)")
   $ cal(O)_m ⊗ D_1 tilde.equiv nu \( M \[ m \] ⊗ M \[ 1 \] \) arrow.r^(nu C_sigma) nu M \[ T_sigma \] arrow.r
     U_E^oo . $ <eq:prism-simplex-cylinder>]
  #emph[Compatibility with simplex operators.] We next verify compatibility with #emph[all] simplex operators.
    Let $alpha : \[ q \] arrow.r \[ m \]$ be monotone and set $rho = sigma alpha$.
    There is an order embedding $j : T_rho arrow.r.hook T_sigma$. We have an equality of whole directed chain maps
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.6)")
   $ M \( j \) C_rho = C_sigma #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] M \( alpha \) ⊗ upright("id")_(M \[ 1 \])
     #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:prism-naturality>]
  To check it, take a basis tuple $\[ a_0 \, dots.h \, a_p \]$ in $M \[ q \]$.
    If $alpha$ is injective on this tuple, both sides of (3.6), on tensoring with $e_0$, $e_1$, or $u$,
    are exactly the same tuple in (3.3), selected by the indices $alpha \( a_0 \) \, dots.h \, alpha \( a_p \)$.
    If $alpha$ is not injective on it, the right side is zero by normalization.
    On the left, equal selected indices yield repeated $f$-images in the $e_0$ and $u$ prescriptions,
    and repeated $g$-images in the $e_1$ prescription, so all three images are zero.
    These cases include every face, every degeneracy, and every composite of them,
    also when $sigma$ itself has repeated vertices. Naturality of the strong Steiner comparison and functoriality
    of $nu$ turn (3.6) into an equality of the strict functors (3.5) after mapping into $U_E^oo$.
  The zero chains in this argument retain their lower boundary data after applying $nu$.
    In the cell table (2.2), the entries satisfy $d x_r^plus.minus = x_(r - 1)^(+) - x_(r - 1)^(-)$.
    If the top entry of a $q$-cell is zero, with $q > 0$, its two $\( q - 1 \)$-entries therefore coincide;
    the resulting cell is the identity on that common $\( q - 1 \)$-cell. That lower cell need not be an identity.
    For example, take $E = { a < b }$, $A = { a }$, $f$ constant at $a$, and $g = upright("id")$.
    The degree-two tensor generator has image $h \( a \, b \) = \[ a \, a \, b \] = 0$,
    but its associated square maps to the identity $2$-cell on the nonidentity edge $\[ a \, b \]$.
    Equation (3.6) compares every degree of the chain maps, so it establishes agreement on these retained
    boundaries as well.
  #emph[Assembly and stationarity.] The oriental presentation and cocontinuity of the Gray tensor in its first
    variable now give
  $ U_E^oo ⊗ D_1 tilde.equiv "colim"_(sigma : Delta \[ m \] arrow.r N E) \( cal(O)_m ⊗ D_1 \) . $
  The compatible maps (3.5) consequently induce $P_(f \, g)^oo : U_E^oo ⊗ D_1 arrow.r U_E^oo$.
    This colimit is taken in strict $omega$-categories; the argument uses tensor cocontinuity,
    not preservation of arbitrary colimits by $nu$. The two endpoint maps are $U^oo \( f \)$ and $U^oo \( g \)$ by
    their simplexwise formulas.
  On a simplex entirely in $A$, the maps $F$ and $G$ coincide and $h \( w \) = 0$ for every selected tuple,
    because $f \( w_p \) = g \( w_p \)$. More precisely, the entire chain map is
  $ C_sigma = F compose \( upright("id")_(M \[ m \]) ⊗ epsilon.alt \) \, wide epsilon.alt \( e_0 \) =
    epsilon.alt \( e_1 \) = 1 \, quad epsilon.alt \( u \) = 0 \, $
  where the chain augmentation of the interval is denoted by the same letter as its categorical realization.
    Applying $nu$ and the simplex colimit proves strict stationarity on $U_A^oo$ relative to $U^oo \( v \)$.
    It is this factorization, rather than the vanishing of individual top chains alone, that proves stationarity.
  #emph[Finite-dimensional reflection.] For finite $n$, let $q_E : U_E^oo arrow.r U_E$ be the reflection unit.
    By Lemma 2, the composite $q_E P_(f \, g)^oo$ factors uniquely as
  $ U_E^oo ⊗ D_1 arrow.r^(q_E ⊗ upright("id")) U_E ⊗ D_1 arrow.r^(P_(f \, g)) U_E . $
  Its endpoint identities follow by precomposing with $q_E$ and using the universal property of reflection.
    To check (3.1), precompose both sides with $q_A ⊗ upright("id")$.
    The resulting equality is the stationarity just proved before reflection.
    The injectivity in the natural bijection of Lemma 2, with target $U_E$,
    gives the claimed equality after reflection. For $n = oo$ no reflection is needed.
    If $E$ or $A$ is empty, the corresponding colimit is empty and the same statements hold with the unique maps
    from it. Finally Lemma 4 gives the stated simplicial homotopy and its stationarity. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 3.2.])[
  (Gluing a stationary cylinder).  #emph[Let $i : B_0 arrow.r B$ and $a : B_0 arrow.r
    X$ be arbitrary strict functors of $n$-categories, and form the pushout
#cetz.canvas({
  import cetz.draw: *
  content((0, 0), [$B_0$])
  content((1.794, 0), [$B$])
  content((0, -1.239), [$X$])
  content((1.794, -1.239), [$Y .$])
  line((0.468, 0), (1.458, 0), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((0.9628, 0.2266), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$i$]])
  line((0, -0.2725), (0, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((-0.3223, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$a$]])
  line((1.794, -0.2725), (1.794, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((2.116, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$b$]])
  line((0.336, -1.239), (1.392, -1.239), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((0.8638, -1.465), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$j$]])
})

 Suppose $F_0 \, F_1 : B arrow.r B$ satisfy $F_0 i = F_1 i = i$, and suppose $P : B ⊗ D_1 arrow.r
   B$ has endpoints $F_0 \, F_1$ and satisfies $P \( i ⊗ upright("id") \) = i pi_(B_0)$.
   Then there is a cylinder $Q : Y ⊗ D_1 arrow.r Y$ stationary on $X$, whose endpoints $overline(F)_0 \,
   overline(F)_1 : Y arrow.r Y$ are specified by
$ overline(F)_delta j = j \, wide overline(F)_delta b = b F_delta wide \( delta = 0 \, 1 \) . $

 Its restriction along $b ⊗ upright("id")$ is $b P$. This construction is natural in $a : B_0 arrow.r X$,
   and its nerve gives a simplicial homotopy between $N_n overline(F)_0$ and $N_n
   overline(F)_1$ stationary along $N_n j$. In particular it applies to Lemma 7 whenever $f \|_A = g \|_A =
   upright("id")_A$, for every attaching functor $U_A arrow.r X$.]
] <lem:pushout-cylinder>

#proof[
  Let $H \( Y \)$ be the right internal hom representing strict functors $\( - \) ⊗ D_1 arrow.r Y$.
    It is an $n$-category by Lemma 2; for $n = oo$ this is automatic. The cylinders
  $ b P : B ⊗ D_1 arrow.r Y \, wide j pi_X : X ⊗ D_1 arrow.r Y $
  transpose to maps $B arrow.r H \( Y \)$ and $X arrow.r H \( Y \)$ in $n upright("Cat")$.
    Their restrictions to $B_0$ agree, since on its cylinder both give
  $ b i pi_(B_0) = j a pi_(B_0) . $
  The pushout universal property in $n upright("Cat")$ thus gives a unique map $Y arrow.r
    H \( Y \)$ extending both transposes. Its transpose is $Q$.
    The endpoint formulas, the equality $Q \( j ⊗ upright("id") \) = j pi_X$,
    and the restriction to $B$ follow from naturality of the adjunction and the same pushout universal property.
    For a map $X arrow.r X'$ commuting with the attaching maps, the induced map $Y arrow.r
    Y'$ carries this cylinder to the corresponding cylinder: the two maps $Y arrow.r
    H \( Y' \)$ agree on $X$ and $B$ and hence agree. This proves naturality.
    Lemma 4 proves the final homotopy assertion. No injectivity, freeness, or cofibrancy hypothesis on $i$, $a$,
    or $X$ entered this construction. ◻
]

== 4 Labelled pieces of algebraic pushouts <sec:pieces>

We fix $1 lt.eq n lt.eq oo$ throughout this section. The arguments concern actual subcategories and actual
  pushouts. This precision will allow us to apply the simplicial gluing lemma even when an attaching functor
  identifies objects or cells.

The sieve and nerve constructions have established antecedents in #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2014"))
  Propositions 5.3 and 5.5. We give the calculations explicitly to identify the labels,
  fibers and intersections after an arbitrary attachment.

A full sub-$n$-category of $C$ on a set of objects $S$ has exactly those objects and contains every
  positive-dimensional cell whose $0$-source and $0$-target belong to $S$.
  It is a #emph[sieve] if a $1$-cell $x arrow.r y$ with $y in S$ forces $x in S$,
  and a #emph[cosieve] if a $1$-cell $x arrow.r y$ with $x in S$ forces $y in S$.
  A sieve is equivalently the fiber over $0$ of a strict functor $C arrow.r D_1$;
  a cosieve is equivalently the fiber over $1$. Indeed, the indicated object assignment extends uniquely to a
  strict functor: each nonempty hom in the target is terminal,
  and the sieve or cosieve condition excludes an arrow from a $1$-labelled object to a $0$-labelled object.
  These fibers are full, since all cells between objects with the same label necessarily map to identities at that
  label.

The following fiber adjoints are those of #cite(label("AraMaltsiniotis2014")) Lemma 2.8.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  (Fibers and objects preserve colimits).  #emph[For $epsilon.alt in { 0 \, 1 }$, the functor
$ F_epsilon.alt : n upright("Cat") \/ D_1 arrow.r n upright("Cat") \,
  wide \( C arrow.r^p D_1 \) mapsto C_epsilon.alt = p^(- 1) \( epsilon.alt \) \, $

 preserves all small colimits. The object functor $"Ob" : n upright("Cat") arrow.r
   upright("Set")$ also preserves all small colimits. Both assertions hold directly for $n = oo$.]
] <lem:fibers>

#proof[
  We construct a right adjoint $Q_0$ to $F_0$. Given $B$, put all its objects over $0$ and adjoin a new object $*$
    over $1$. Keep the hom categories $B \( b \, b' \)$, and set
  $ Q_0 \( B \) \( b \, * \) = upright(bold(1)) \, wide Q_0 \( B \) \( * \, b \) = diameter \, wide
    Q_0 \( B \) \( * \, * \) = upright(bold(1)) . $
  Here the homs are strict $\( n - 1 \)$-categories for finite $n$, sets when $n = 1$,
    and strict $omega$-categories when $n = oo$; $upright(bold(1))$ and $diameter$ denote their terminal and
    initial objects. Compositions inside $B$ are retained. Every remaining composition either has empty domain or
    has terminal codomain, so is uniquely determined. Associativity, units,
    and interchange hold by the laws in $B$ or by uniqueness.
    This defines $Q_0 \( B \)$ and its map to $D_1$ in all the stated dimensions.
  For $p : C arrow.r D_1$, a functor $C_0 arrow.r B$ extends uniquely over $D_1$ to $C arrow.r Q_0 \( B \)$.
    All objects of $C_1$ must go to $*$; their internal cells and the cells from $C_0$ to $C_1$ have unique images
    in the specified terminal homs. There are no cells from $C_1$ to $C_0$,
    since their $1$-boundaries would map to an arrow $1 arrow.r 0$.
    Restriction and extension are inverse and natural, giving
  $ "Hom"_(n upright("Cat") \/ D_1) \( C \, Q_0 \( B \) \) tilde.equiv "Hom"_(n upright("Cat")) \( C_0 \, B \) . $
  Thus $F_0$ is a left adjoint. For $F_1$, place $B$ over $1$ and adjoin a new initial object over $0$,
    with terminal homs from that object into $B$, empty reverse homs, and terminal endomorphism hom.
    The same restriction argument gives a right adjoint $Q_1$.
    In particular these constructions include $B = diameter$ and prove preservation of the empty colimit as well.
  For a set $S$, let $K \( S \)$ be the ordinary chaotic category on $S$,
    with one arrow for each ordered pair of objects, regarded as an $n$-category with identity cells above
    dimension one. Every object function $"Ob" C arrow.r S$ has a unique strict extension $C arrow.r K \( S \)$.
    Consequently $"Ob"$ is left adjoint to $K$ and preserves colimits. ◻
]

The next elementary property of orientals is needed before taking a colimit of simplex pieces.
  It is not enough merely to know their sets of vertices.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  (Full oriental segments).  #emph[Let $I$ be an initial or a final segment of $\[ m \] = { 0 \, dots.h \, m }$.
    If $I$ is nonempty and has $q + 1$ vertices, the face inclusion identifies $tau_n
    cal(O)_q$ with the full sub-$n$-category of $tau_n cal(O)_m$ on $I$.
    If $I$ is empty, that full subcategory is empty. Here and below $tau_n = upright("id")$ when $n = oo$.]
] <lem:oriental-segments>

#proof[
  Recall the oriental presentation from Section 2: its atoms are indexed by strictly increasing tuples,
    with $0$-source the first vertex and $0$-target the last vertex.
    All cells are finite composites and identities of these atoms.
    In particular every $1$-cell has nondecreasing endpoints.
  Suppose a cell has $0$-source $a$ and $0$-target $b$. In any expression for it,
    every occurring atom has all its vertices in $\[ a \, b \]$. This follows by induction on the expression.
    For an atom it is the tuple description. In a $0$-composite the factors have endpoints $\( a \, c \)$ and
    $\( c \, b \)$, with $a lt.eq c lt.eq b$. In a composition in positive dimension both factors have the same
    $0$-endpoints as the composite. Identities preserve these endpoints,
    and an identity on a vertex has support at that vertex. These observations prove the induction.
    If $a \, b in I$, the whole interval $\[ a \, b \]$ lies in $I$,
    so the expression uses only the atoms of that face. Thus the face generates every cell on those objects.
  For finite $n gt.eq 1$, reflection leaves objects unchanged and every cell of dimension at most $n$ is
    represented by a cell before reflection. The same argument applied to a representative proves surjectivity
    onto the full subcategory after reflection; higher cells are identities.
    For injectivity, the inclusion $I arrow.r.hook \[ m \]$ has a monotone retraction:
    clamp to the last vertex of $I$ for an initial segment, or to its first vertex for a final segment.
    By cosimplicial functoriality it induces a retraction on orientals, and then on their reflections.
    The face map is therefore injective on every cell set. This proves the full-subcategory assertion.
    If $I$ is empty there are no objects and hence no cells; no retraction is needed. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 4.3.])[
  (Poset pieces).  #emph[There is a canonical identification $"Ob" U_P = P$ for every poset $P$.
    If $S subset P$ is a sieve, the map $U_S arrow.r U_P$ is the inclusion of the full $0$-fiber of the membership
    label $U_P arrow.r D_1$. If $S$ is a cosieve, it is the inclusion of the full $1$-fiber.
    These identifications are natural for maps of labelled posets and include empty posets and empty fibers.]
] <lem:poset-pieces>

#proof[
  Use the canonical simplex presentation
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
   $ U_P = "colim"_(sigma : Delta \[ m \] arrow.r N P) tau_n cal(O)_m . $ <eq:poset-simplex-colimit>]
  By Lemma 9, its object set is the colimit of the vertex sets $\[ m \]$.
    Sending the $j$th vertex of a simplex $sigma$ to $sigma \( j \)$ identifies this colimit with $P$:
    each element occurs as a zero-simplex, and the face inclusions from zero-simplices identify every vertex with
    that occurrence.
  For a sieve $S$, its characteristic map $P arrow.r \[ 1 \]$ assigns $0$ on $S$ and $1$ on its complement.
    It is monotone and induces the displayed label on $U_P$, since $U_(\[ 1 \]) = D_1$.
    The presentation (4.1) is consequently a colimit in $n upright("Cat") \/ D_1$ as well:
    slice colimits have the underlying colimits in $n upright("Cat")$.
    In its $sigma$-piece the vertices mapping to $S$ form an initial segment $I_sigma$.
    By Lemma 10, the $0$-fiber of that piece is its truncated segment oriental.
    Taking fibers commutes with the colimit by Lemma 9.
  We verify that this colimit of segment orientals is canonically $U_S$.
    Restricting each nonempty simplex segment to $S$ gives a map from its segment oriental to $U_S$;
    the empty segment has the unique such map. These maps are compatible with every simplex operator.
    In the reverse direction, each simplex entirely in $S$ supplies its whole oriental to the fiber colimit.
    The two composites are identities. On a simplex of $S$ this is immediate.
    On a nonempty segment of an arbitrary simplex $sigma$, it follows from the face inclusion selecting $I_sigma$,
    which is a morphism in the simplex category of $N P$; on an empty segment there is nothing to check.
    The isomorphism so obtained is the canonical map $U_S arrow.r U_P$, followed into its fiber.
    Fibers are full sieves, which proves the claim. The argument for a cosieve uses label $1$ on $S$,
    final vertex segments, and $F_1$ throughout. All constructions commute with maps preserving the labels. ◻
]

Here is the form of the fiber calculation that we will use in pushouts.
  It also records precisely which hypotheses are needed of a label on an arbitrary attaching target.

#thm([Proposition 4.4.])[
  (Labelled attachments).  #emph[Let $A subset P$ be a full subposet, let $a : U_A arrow.r
    X$ be any strict functor, and form
$ Y = X product.co_(U_A) U_P . $

 Suppose a monotone map $lambda : P arrow.r \[ 1 \]$ and a strict functor $xi : X arrow.r D_1$ satisfy
$ xi a = U \( lambda \|_A \) : U_A arrow.r D_1 \, wide U_(\[ 1 \]) = D_1 . $

 Write $P_epsilon.alt = lambda^(- 1) \( epsilon.alt \)$, $A_epsilon.alt = A ∩ P_epsilon.alt$, and $X_epsilon.alt =
   xi^(- 1) \( epsilon.alt \)$. Then the induced label $Y arrow.r D_1$ has actual full fibers
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ Y_epsilon.alt tilde.equiv X_epsilon.alt product.co_(U_(A_epsilon.alt)) U_(P_epsilon.alt) . $ <eq:pushout-fiber>]

 Moreover, independently of these labels,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
 $ "Ob" Y tilde.equiv "Ob" X product.co_A P tilde.equiv "Ob" X product.co \( P \\ A \) . $ <eq:pushout-objects>]

 For two such pairs of compatible labels, write $macron(lambda)_1 \, macron(lambda)_2 : Y arrow.r
   D_1$ for the induced labels. Their $0$-fibers intersect in the $0$-fiber of $max
   compose \( macron(lambda)_1 \, macron(lambda)_2 \)$; their $1$-fibers intersect in the $1$-fiber of $min
   compose \( macron(lambda)_1 \, macron(lambda)_2 \)$. Formula (4.2),
   using the corresponding maximum or minimum on both original legs, computes these intersections as well.]
] <prop:labelled-pushout>

#proof[
  The compatibility assumption makes the defining pushout a pushout in $n upright("Cat") \/ D_1$.
    Applying $F_epsilon.alt$ and then Lemmas 9 and 11 gives (4.2).
    This identifies the canonical map from the displayed pushout to $Y$ with the inclusion of a full fiber.
    The object formula follows from the object part of Lemma 9 and Lemma 11.
    Its last isomorphism uses only the injectivity of $A arrow.r.hook P$:
    old vertices of $P$ are replaced by their images in $X$, while vertices in $P \\ A$ remain distinct new
    objects. There is no injectivity requirement on $"Ob" a$.
  Two labels on $Y$ give a functor $Y arrow.r D_1 times D_1$.
    The monotone functions $max \, min : \[ 1 \] times \[ 1 \] arrow.r \[ 1 \]$ are strict functors,
    so their composites are labels on $Y$. An object has maximum label $0$ exactly when both labels are $0$,
    and minimum label $1$ exactly when both labels are $1$. Full subcategories on the same objects are equal,
    which proves the assertions about intersections. The composite labels restrict to the corresponding maximum or
    minimum labels on both legs of the original pushout, so the same fiber formula applies. ◻
]

For use in the next two sections, we spell out both cover consequences.

#thm([Corollary 4.5.])[
  (Sieve and cosieve covers after attachment).  #emph[With the notation of Proposition 12,
    the following are canonical identifications of subcategories of $Y$.]
  + #emph[Suppose $P_1 \, P_2$ are sieves of $P$, contain $A$, and cover $P$. Then the subcategories
$ Y_i = X product.co_(U_A) U_(P_i) quad \( i = 1 \, 2 \) $

 are sieves covering $Y$ on objects, and
$ Y_1 ∩ Y_2 = X product.co_(U_A) U_(P_1 ∩ P_2) . $

]
  + #emph[Suppose $T_1 \, T_2$ are cosieves of $P$, cover $P$, satisfy $A subset T_1$, and satisfy $A ∩ T_2 =
    diameter$. Then
$ Y_1 = X product.co_(U_A) U_(T_1) \, wide Y_2 = U_(T_2) $

 are cosieves covering $Y$ on objects, and $Y_1 ∩ Y_2 = U_(T_1 ∩ T_2)$.]
  #emph[If $A$ itself is a sieve of $P$, the canonical map $X arrow.r Y$ is the inclusion of a full sieve.]
] <cor:attachment-covers>

#proof[
  For (i), label membership in each $P_i$ by $0$ and label every object of $X$ by $0$.
    Apply Proposition 12 to the two labels and their maximum.
    For (ii), label membership in each $T_i$ by $1$; use constant label $1$ on $X$ for $T_1$ and constant label
    $0$ on $X$ for $T_2$. These agree on the attaching source by the stated conditions.
    The $1$-fiber of the second old-side label is empty, and so is that of the minimum of the two old-side labels.
    The same is true on their attaching sources. Formula (4.2) therefore gives $U_(T_2)$ and $U_(T_1 ∩ T_2)$
    exactly. In both cases (4.3) proves the object cover. Finally, when $A$ is a sieve,
    label $A$ and $X$ by $0$ and $P \\ A$ by $1$. The $0$-fiber formula is $X product.co_(U_A) U_A = X$. ◻
]

In particular, an attaching functor may identify two old objects, collapse an old arrow to an identity,
  or identify parallel old cells. These operations cause no ambiguity in the cover calculations:
  the labels agree on every old cell before the pushout is taken.
  The fiber adjunctions compute the resulting subcategories after all those identifications.

#thm([Lemma 4.6.])[
  (Street nerves of two-piece covers).  #emph[Let $C_1 \, C_2 subset
    C$ be two full sieves which cover the objects of $C$, or two full cosieves which cover its objects.
    Put $C_12 = C_1 ∩ C_2$. The square
#cetz.canvas({
  import cetz.draw: *
  content((0, 0), [$N_n C_12$])
  content((2.454, 0), [$N_n C_2$])
  content((0, -1.239), [$N_n C_1$])
  content((2.454, -1.239), [$N_n C$])
  line((0.798, 0), (1.722, 0), mark: (end: ">"))
  line((0.798, 0), (0.798, 0.3))
  line((0, -0.2725), (0, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  line((0, -0.2725), (0.3, -0.2725))
  line((2.454, -0.2725), (2.454, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  line((2.454, -0.2725), (2.754, -0.2725))
  line((0.732, -1.239), (1.854, -1.239), mark: (end: ">"))
  line((0.732, -1.239), (0.732, -0.9386))
})

 is an ordinary pushout of simplicial sets. All four maps are monomorphisms,
   so it is also a homotopy pushout in the Kan–Quillen model structure.]
] <lem:nerve-cover>

#proof[
  A full inclusion induces a monomorphism of Street nerves, and the intersection of the two nerves is $N_n C_12$:
    a functor whose image belongs to both subcategories has a unique factorization through their full
    intersection. It remains to prove that every simplex $z : cal(O)_m arrow.r
    C$ belongs to one of the two nerves. In the sieve case choose a piece containing $z \( m \)$.
    For each vertex $j lt.eq m$, the oriental has an arrow $j arrow.r m$,
    whose image shows that $z \( j \)$ belongs to that piece.
    Fullness then puts every cell of the simplex in the same piece.
    In the cosieve case choose a piece containing $z \( 0 \)$ and use the arrows $0 arrow.r j$.
    This includes $m = 0$ and proves the union statement degree by degree.
    A union of simplicial subsets is their ordinary pushout over the intersection.
    Since its legs are monomorphisms, the homotopy-pushout assertion follows from Lemma 6. ◻
]

== 5 Couniversality and the unit on posets <sec:couniversality>

Fix $n in { 1 \, 2 \, dots.h } union { oo }$ throughout this section.
  A #emph[Thomason equivalence] is a strict functor whose Street nerve is a weak homotopy equivalence.
  This terminology uses no model structure on $n upright("Cat")$.
  We prove that certain poset inclusions remain Thomason equivalences after every pushout,
  and then establish the adjunction unit on posets independently of transfer.

For an inclusion $i : A arrow.r.hook E$ of posets, a right adjoint retraction $r : E arrow.r A$ satisfies

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.1)")
 $ a lt.eq e quad arrow.l.r.double quad a lt.eq r \( e \) wide \( a in A \, med e in E \) \, wide r i =
   upright("id")_A . $ <eq:poset-adjunction>]

We identify $A$ with its image in $E$. In particular $r \( e \) lt.eq e$.
  The inclusion is a #emph[sieve] when $e lt.eq a in A$ implies $e in A$.
  For an arbitrary strict functor $a : U_A arrow.r X$, write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.2)")
 $ Y = X product.co_(U_A) U_E \, wide j : X arrow.r Y \, wide v : U_E arrow.r Y . $ <eq:couniversal-pushout>]

The symbol $a$ here denotes the attaching functor; elements of $A$ will be denoted by $b$ below.

#thm([Lemma 5.1.])[
  (A greatest new element).  #emph[Suppose that $A arrow.r.hook E$ is a sieve with a right adjoint retraction,
    and that $E \\ A$ has a greatest element. For every attaching functor in (5.2),
    the map $j$ admits a retraction $p : Y arrow.r X$ such that $N_n \( j p \)$ and $upright("id")_(N_n Y)$ are
    joined by a zigzag of simplicial homotopies. Consequently $j$ is a Thomason equivalence.
    Neither $A$ nor $E$ needs to be finite.]
] <lem:greatest-new>

#proof[
  Let $x$ be greatest in $E \\ A$ and put $t = r \( x \) in A$.
    If $b in A$, $y in E \\ A$, and $b lt.eq y$, then $b lt.eq y lt.eq x$, so (5.1) gives $b lt.eq t$.
    Define maps $k \, g : E arrow.r E$ by
  $ k \( e \) = cases(delim: "{", e \, & e in A \,, t \, & e in.not A \,) wide g \( e \) =
    cases(delim: "{", e \, & e in A \,, x \, & e in.not A .) $
  Both are monotone. Indeed, for a comparable pair $e lt.eq e'$,
    the old–old case is unchanged and the new–new case is constant.
    In the old–new case the preceding observation gives $e lt.eq t lt.eq x$.
    The new–old case cannot occur because $A$ is a sieve. We therefore have pointwise inequalities
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.3)")
   $ upright("id")_E lt.eq g \, wide k lt.eq g \, $ <eq:local-zigzag>]
  and all three maps restrict to $upright("id")_A$. The common upper map $g$ is constant on $E \\ A$,
    exactly as required in Lemma 7. Figure 1 shows why this common upper map is useful:
    $upright("id")_E$ and $k$ need not be pointwise comparable, although both lie below $g$.
  Apply Lemma 8 to the two comparisons in (5.3). Let $G \, K : Y arrow.r
    Y$ be the pushout endomorphisms whose restrictions to $X$ are $j$ and whose restrictions to $U_E$ are $v thin
    c_n N \( g \)$ and $v thin c_n N \( k \)$, respectively. The resulting right Gray cylinders,
    both stationary on $X$, have endpoint pattern
  $ upright("id")_Y med arrow.r med G med arrow.l med K . $
  By Lemma 4, these give simplicial homotopies with the same endpoint pattern on Street nerves.
  The image of $k$ lies in $A$, so $k = i macron(k)$ for a monotone retraction $macron(k) : E arrow.r A$.
    The maps $upright("id")_X$ and $a thin c_n N \( macron(k) \) : U_E arrow.r
    X$ agree on $U_A$ and hence define $p : Y arrow.r X$. They give $p j = upright("id")_X$.
    Comparing restrictions to the two pushout summands gives $j p = K$:
    on $X$ both restrict to $j$, and on $U_E$ one has
  $ j p thin v = j thin a thin c_n N \( macron(k) \) = v thin c_n N \( i \) thin c_n N \( macron(k) \) =
    v thin c_n N \( k \) . $
  After realization, concatenate the homotopy from $upright("id")_Y$ to $G$ with the reverse of the homotopy from
    $j p$ to $G$. Thus $\| N_n p \|$ is a homotopy inverse to $\| N_n j \|$, proving the assertion.
    This reversal is a reversal of an ordinary topological homotopy; no reversed Gray cylinder is asserted. ◻
]

