) <fig:local-diamond>

#thm([Theorem 5.2.])[
  (Couniversality).  #emph[If $i : A arrow.r.hook E$ is a sieve of small posets with a right adjoint retraction,
    then $c_n N \( i \)$ is a couniversal Thomason equivalence: for every $a : U_A arrow.r X$, the canonical map
$ X arrow.r X product.co_(U_A) U_E $

 is a Thomason equivalence. This holds for every finite $n gt.eq 1$ and for $n = oo$.]
] <thm:couniversal>

#proof[
  First suppose that $E$ is finite. We use induction on $\| E \\ A \|$,
    simultaneously for all attaching targets $X$ and all attaching functors $a$.
    When this cardinality is zero, $j$ is an isomorphism. When the complement has a greatest element, Lemma 15 applies.
  Otherwise the nonempty finite poset $E \\ A$ has at least two distinct maximal elements $x_1 \, x_2$:
    a finite poset with a unique maximal element has that element as its greatest element.
    Each $x_i$ is also maximal in $E$, since an element above a new element cannot belong to the sieve $A$. Put
  $ E_1 = E \\ { x_1 } \, wide E_2 = E \\ { x_2 } \, wide E_12 = E \\ { x_1 \, x_2 } . $
  These are sieves of $E$ containing $A$; moreover $E_1 union E_2 = E$ and $E_1 ∩ E_2 = E_12$.
    The restriction of $r$ to each $E_T$, for $T = 1 \, 2 \, 12$, still takes values in $A$ and satisfies (5.1).
    Each complement $E_T \\ A$ is smaller. Define
  $ Y_T = X product.co_(U_A) U_(E_T) \, wide j_T : X arrow.r Y_T . $
  By induction every $N_n j_T$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
  Corollary 13(i), applied to the sieves $E_1 \, E_2$ containing $A$ and covering $E$,
    identifies the canonical maps $Y_i arrow.r Y$ as full sieve inclusions.
    Their full intersection is canonically $Y_12$, and they cover the objects of $Y$.
    These identifications hold for the given arbitrary attaching functor $a$.
  Lemma 14 now identifies the bottom pushout in the following diagram with $N_n Y$:
  (5.4)
  #cetz.canvas({
  import cetz.draw: *
  content((0, 0), [$N_n X$])
  content((2.915, 0), [$N_n X$])
  content((5.963, 0), [$N_n X$])
  content((0, -1.239), [$N_n Y_1$])
  content((2.915, -1.239), [$N_n Y_12$])
  content((5.963, -1.239), [$N_n Y_2 .$])
  line((0, -0.2725), (0, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((-0.6114, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$N_n j_1$]])
  line((2.315, -0.11), (0.6, -0.11))
  line((2.315, 0.11), (0.6, 0.11))
  line((3.515, 0.11), (5.363, 0.11))
  line((3.515, -0.11), (5.363, -0.11))
  line((2.915, -0.2725), (2.915, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((3.575, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$N_n j_12$]])
  line((5.963, -0.2725), (5.963, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((6.574, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$N_n j_2$]])
  line((2.117, -1.239), (0.732, -1.239), mark: (end: ">"))
